{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789166197,"nonce":"9IltHNnz8zJ6v9kbJ15G4Pht+qw=","message":"Fixed in 83dd1c2. ash_bench.py splits a sample at its widest gap when both sides have at least two samples and the gap is at least 10% of the fast side's median, and records modes.fast/modes.slow with their medians and counts. The console table prints 'bimodal 3x886/4x1187', the site marks the row and gives both modes in the tooltip, and the CI leg emits a notice. A baseline comparison compares fast mode against fast mode and notes the split, so a change in the draw no longer reads as a regression. merge_bench_site.py applies the same split to lanes that only keep samples (hl_bench, aot_bench), so the JVM and HashLink rows get it too.\n\nOn sweep 34387507221, the one in this issue: nbody 4x927.8/3x1273.5, mandelbrot 5x677.8/2x808.7, deltablue 5x104.9/2x133.8. The in-sweep A/B that answers the underlying question is d84cc02.","files":null}]}